# ─── Deanonymization surfaces ──────────────────────────────────────────
# sip-bridge/ routes PSTN calls and handles caller phone numbers;
# signal-notifier/ does HMAC-hashed contact resolution. Both are top-level,
# NOT under apps/ despite what older docs say.
sip-bridge/                         @rhonda-rodododo
signal-notifier/                    @rhonda-rodododo

# ─── The fleet's own trust base ────────────────────────────────────────
# A worker that edits the orchestrator, the fragment defining its own lane's
# write scope, or the hook enforcing the write-deny rules can widen its own
# authority. These must never be self-approvable.
orchestrator/                       @rhonda-rodododo
.claude/agents/fragments/           @rhonda-rodododo
.claude/settings.json               @rhonda-rodododo
CODEOWNERS                          @rhonda-rodododo

# ─── Supply chain, release, deployment ─────────────────────────────────
.github/workflows/                  @rhonda-rodododo
knope.toml                          @rhonda-rodododo
deploy/                             @rhonda-rodododo

# ─── Security-relevant test suites, and ONLY those ─────────────────────
# Narrower than #615's blanket `tests/`: weakening a test is the shortest
# route to disabling the check that would have caught the change, but that
# only matters for the two directories where the test IS the guard.
# tests/orchestrator/ is the fleet judging itself; tests/mocks/ is the Tauri
# IPC mock, which mirrors the Rust CryptoState and is therefore a key-boundary
# file. Every other test directory is ordinary lane work and merges on green —
# a blanket `tests/` would have held every BDD feature PR the backend and
# desktop lanes write.
tests/orchestrator/                 @rhonda-rodododo
tests/mocks/                        @rhonda-rodododo

# ─── Key material, wherever it appears ─────────────────────────────────
# Mirrors SECRET_PATH_PATTERNS (orchestrator/src/config.ts). checkScope
